===========================================================================
INTRODUCTION : THE ATTACK HAS CHANGED
===========================================================================

The previous generation of this project treated the Steckerbrett as the main
battlefield. A partial key was refined by an evolutionary search, judged by
German-language structure, and converted into readable text by a segmentation
stage.

Version 16 changes the order of operations.

The program no longer asks the Genetic Algorithm to wander through the entire
cryptographic wilderness. It first constructs a narrow corridor through that
wilderness by combining a crib, the Enigma no-self-encryption property, rotor
simulation, symmetric plugboard constraints, contradiction propagation, and a
native multithreaded search across the machine's core settings.

Only after the Bombe-inspired stage has reduced the space does the linguistic
engine rank Ringstellung candidates. Only after that does the mutation-based
evolutionary stage refine the remaining unknown plugboard connections. The
final candidate is then exposed to a human review loop, segmented by a
Viterbi-style dynamic program, and exported as a simulated historical dossier.

The v16 pipeline is therefore not one algorithm. It is a relay race:

  Crib Hypothesis
        |
        v
  No-Self-Encryption Filter
        |
        v
  Parallel Bombe-Inspired Constraint Search
        |
        v
  Ringstellung Language Ranking
        |
        v
  Mutation-Based Plugboard Evolution
        |
        v
  Human Override
        |
        v
  Viterbi-Style Word Segmentation
        |
        v
  Historical-Style PDF Telemetry

This paper documents that exact implementation. Where the code uses a
heuristic rather than a formal probabilistic model, the heuristic is described
as a heuristic. Where the code is inspired by the historical Bombe rather than
a cycle-accurate reconstruction of it, the distinction is explicit.

The objective is fidelity, not mythology.

===========================================================================
CHAPTER I : THE MACHINE MODEL AND THE ATTACK BOUNDARY
===========================================================================

### 1.1 Supported Enigma Configuration

The cryptographic core models a three-rotor Enigma using:

* Rotors I, II, III, IV, and V.
* Any ordered selection of three distinct rotors.
* Reflector B.
* A 26-letter alphabet, A through Z.
* A symmetric plugboard mapping applied before and after the rotor core.
* Ring settings and starting positions represented as three letters.
* Rotor stepping before each encrypted or decrypted character.

The rotor-order count is the permutation:

  P(5,3) = 5 x 4 x 3 = 60

The program automatically generates all sixty Walzenlagen. No manual rotor
order is required from the operator.

### 1.2 Electrical Transformation

Let S be the Steckerbrett permutation and let E_k be the internal Enigma core
permutation at character position k, after stepping. For plaintext letter p_k,
the ciphertext letter c_k is:

  c_k = S( E_k( S(p_k) ) )

Because the plugboard is an involution, S(S(x)) = x. Applying S to both sides
gives the central constraint used by the Bombe-inspired stage:

  S(c_k) = E_k( S(p_k) )

This equation is the bridge between a guessed plaintext fragment and the
unknown plugboard.

### 1.3 Rotor Stepping

The FastEnigmaCore performs the familiar three-rotor stepping pattern:

* The right rotor always advances.
* If the right rotor is at its notch, the middle rotor advances.
* If the middle rotor is at its notch, both middle and left rotors advance.

The stepping occurs before each character traverses the machine. This detail is
not decorative. An off-by-one stepping error transforms every derived
permutation into poison.

### 1.4 What This Build Does Not Search

The implementation is deliberately bounded. It does not model every historical
Enigma variant, every reflector, four-rotor naval configurations, multiple
notches beyond those defined for the selected rotors, or a historically exact
Bombe electromechanical architecture.

It is an academic three-rotor cryptanalysis demonstrator with a hybrid search
pipeline.

===========================================================================
CHAPTER II : CRIB ACQUISITION AND THE NO-SELF-ENCRYPTION FILTER
===========================================================================

### 2.1 The Crib as a Constraint Injection

A crib is a hypothesized plaintext fragment. It does not prove what the message
contains. It injects structure into the search.

Version 16 begins by testing a curated list of thirty-two historically
motivated phrases at Offset 0. These include command headers, weather phrases,
classification markers, routing language, and common military formulae.

Each candidate is treated as a hypothesis. The program attempts them in a fixed
priority order and stops at the first phrase that produces surviving core
configurations.

If every predefined Offset-0 hypothesis fails, the operator supplies a manual
crib.

### 2.2 Enigma's No-Self-Encryption Property

A correctly configured Enigma never maps a letter to itself. Therefore, for a
crib aligned at offset o:

  crib[k] != ciphertext[o + k]

must hold for every k in the crib.

The alignment scanner drags the manual crib across the ciphertext and rejects
any offset containing even one self-letter clash. This is a cheap filter, but
cheap filters are precious when they remove entire branches before the
27-million-state engine wakes up.

### 2.3 Fixed-Offset and Sliding-Offset Modes

The code implements two operational paths:

  [A] Curated crib mode
      The crib is tested only at Offset 0.

  [B] Manual crib mode
      Every offset satisfying the no-self-encryption rule is retained.

The second path is broader, but each additional admissible offset multiplies
the number of constraint checks performed for every core state.

===========================================================================
CHAPTER III : THE 27,418,560-STATE CORE SWEEP
===========================================================================

### 3.1 Decomposing the Search Space

Phase 1 scans:

  60 rotor orders
  x 26 right-rotor ring settings
  x 26^3 starting positions
  ------------------------------------------------
  = 60 x 26 x 17,576
  = 27,418,560 core states

During this phase, the left and middle ring letters are represented as A. The
right ring is explicitly varied. The remaining left and middle Ringstellung
search is deferred to Phase 1.5.

### 3.2 Native Parallelism

The state interval is divided among std::thread workers according to
hardware_concurrency(). If the platform reports zero logical processors, the
program falls back to four threads.

Each worker owns thread-local rotor tables and a thread-local permutation slice.
Shared output is protected by a mutex, while progress is reported through an
atomic state counter.

The architecture is intentionally CPU-native. It avoids creating a heavyweight
Enigma object for every letter of every state.

### 3.3 Precomputed Wiring Tables

When a worker enters a new rotor order, it builds:

* Forward rotor wiring arrays.
* Inverse rotor wiring arrays.
* Notch lookup tables.
* Reflector B as integer indices.

For each character position in the crib, the worker steps the rotor state and
constructs a complete 26-letter permutation for the rotor-reflector-rotor path
without the plugboard.

The result is a sequence:

  E_0, E_1, E_2, ..., E_(L-1)

where L is the crib length and each E_k maps all twenty-six possible input
letters through the internal machine at relative position k.

This changes the inner loop from repeated string searching into indexed table
lookups. The cryptanalytic furnace gets fewer moving parts and more heat.

===========================================================================
CHAPTER IV : BOMBE-INSPIRED CONSTRAINT PROPAGATION
===========================================================================

### 4.1 Seeding the Plugboard Hypothesis

For each core state and crib offset, the program selects the most frequent
letter in the crib as a starting node. Let that letter be p*.

It then tries all twenty-six possible plugboard images:

  S(p*) = g,  where g belongs to {A, ..., Z}

The reverse assignment is installed immediately:

  S(g) = p*

This enforces the plugboard's symmetric, involutive structure.

### 4.2 Propagation Rule

At crib position k, let p be the crib letter and c the aligned ciphertext
letter. The known internal permutation is E_k.

If S(p) is known, then:

  S(c) = E_k( S(p) )

If S(c) is known, the same involutive Enigma relation can be propagated in the
opposite direction:

  S(p) = E_k( S(c) )

The code repeatedly traverses every crib relation until no new assignment can
be produced.

This is a fixed-point computation. Information ricochets through the menu until
it either stabilizes or hits a contradiction.

### 4.3 Contradiction Detection

A branch is rejected when a letter is forced to map to two different targets,
or when the symmetric reverse mapping conflicts with an existing assignment.

Formally, if the branch already contains:

  S(x) = y

and propagation demands:

  S(x) = z,  with y != z

then the branch is impossible under the current rotor state, ring hypothesis,
starting position, crib, and offset.

No language model is needed at this point. The branch dies electrically.

### 4.4 Stop Heuristic

A non-contradictory branch is not automatically accepted. The implementation
requires at least twelve assigned plugboard letters. It then counts how many of
the twenty-six initial guesses produce such a branch.

The core state is retained only when the number of surviving guesses is between
one and five inclusive.

This is a selectivity heuristic:

  assigned plugboard letters >= 12
  and
  1 <= surviving seed guesses <= 5

The program stores the last surviving partial plugboard mapping for that core
state.

### 4.5 Historical Fidelity Statement

This stage is Bombe-inspired. It uses crib menus, Enigma permutations,
involutive plugboard constraints, bidirectional propagation, and contradiction
elimination. It does not emulate the physical drums, relays, diagonal board,
stop circuitry, timing, or complete operational procedure of a wartime Bombe.

The inspiration is mathematical, not museum-grade mechanical reconstruction.

===========================================================================
CHAPTER V : PHASE 1.5 AND THE RINGSTELLUNG EQUIVALENCE
===========================================================================

### 5.1 Why a Second Ring Search Exists

Phase 1 explicitly varies the right-rotor ring setting while the left and middle
ring letters begin at A. Every surviving Bombe candidate therefore carries:

* A rotor order.
* A starting position.
* A right-ring hypothesis.
* A crib offset.
* A partial plugboard mapping.

Phase 1.5 expands each hit across all left and middle ring settings:

  26 x 26 = 676 combinations per hit

The right-ring letter remains the value discovered during Phase 1.

### 5.2 Preserving the Effective Rotor Offset

Rotor wiring depends on the difference between window position and ring
setting. For a rotor position P and ring R, the effective offset is:

  O = P - R  (mod 26)

When the left or middle ring is changed, the program compensates the displayed
starting position so that the effective wiring offset remains equivalent:

  P' = P + R'  (mod 26)

This produces representative keys from an equivalence class of left-rotor and
middle-rotor position/ring pairs that share the same effective initial wiring
offset in the implemented three-rotor model.

### 5.3 Rewinding a Nonzero Crib Offset

A Bombe hit aligned at a nonzero offset describes the machine state at the crib,
not necessarily the state at the beginning of the intercepted message.

For such a hit, Phase 1.5 searches all 17,576 possible initial positions,
steps each candidate forward by the crib offset, and selects a position that
reaches the target rotor state.

This is a direct rewind-by-forward-simulation method. It is computationally
expensive, but it preserves the program's stepping semantics without requiring
an inverse stepping algorithm.

### 5.4 Full-Message Ranking

Each ring candidate decrypts the complete ciphertext using the partial
plugboard recovered by Phase 1. The language fitness engine scores the result.

The highest-scoring candidate across all Bombe hits becomes the core key passed
to Phase 2.

===========================================================================
CHAPTER VI : THE LANGUAGE ORACLE IS A FEATURE ENGINE, NOT A LOG-LIKELIHOOD
===========================================================================

### 6.1 Dictionary Normalization

The embedded Wehrmacht-oriented vocabulary contains thousands of military,
meteorological, naval, aviation, geographic, administrative, cryptographic,
and general German terms.

Before use, every entry is normalized:

* German umlauts become AE, OE, or UE.
* Eszett becomes SS.
* Lowercase ASCII becomes uppercase.
* Every non-A-to-Z byte is discarded.
* Duplicate normalized words are removed.

In the current source, 3,362 literal entries collapse to 2,782 unique normalized
A-to-Z tokens.

The normalized vocabulary is stored both as a vector and as an unordered_set.
The set enables constant-time average dictionary membership tests during word
segmentation.

### 6.2 Dictionary-Derived N-Gram Tables

The MarkovChainEngine does not load statistical probabilities from a corpus.
Instead, it records whether each possible bigram and quadgram appears at least
once inside the normalized dictionary.

The current build activates:

  447 valid bigrams out of 26^2 = 676
  6,081 valid quadgrams out of 26^4 = 456,976

These are binary linguistic features:

  valid_bigram[b]   belongs to {false, true}
  valid_quadgram[q] belongs to {false, true}

Therefore, the score is Markov-inspired in the broad N-gram sense, but it is
not a trained Markov-chain log-likelihood.

### 6.3 Index of Coincidence Gate

For text T of length N with letter counts f_i, the Index of Coincidence is:

                SUM_i f_i (f_i - 1)
  IoC(T) = --------------------------------
                     N (N - 1)

The code rejects a candidate with a score of -5000 when:

  IoC(T) > 0.095

or:

  IoC(T) < 0.035

This gate eliminates extremely repetitive strings and highly noise-like
strings before N-gram rewards can dominate the search.

### 6.4 Exact Fitness Function

For a candidate text T, define:

* B as the set of dictionary-derived valid bigrams.
* Q as the set of dictionary-derived valid quadgrams.
* count_T(x) as the number of occurrences of N-gram x in T.

For a candidate passing the IoC gate, the implemented score is:

  Score(T) = 1000 x IoC(T)

             + 2 x SUM over b in B of MIN(count_T(b), 6)

             + 15 x SUM over q in Q of count_T(q)

The cap of six applies independently to each valid bigram. Quadgram rewards are
not capped.

This design fights the old reward-hacking pattern in two ways:

* IoC rejects implausible global repetition.
* Per-bigram caps prevent a single frequent pair from printing unlimited money.

The engine still remains heuristic. It measures compatibility with a curated
vocabulary, not the probability of authentic German under a statistically
trained corpus model.

===========================================================================
CHAPTER VII : MUTATION-BASED EVOLUTIONARY PLUGBOARD SEARCH
===========================================================================

### 7.1 The Reduced Mission

The Bombe-inspired stage supplies a locked partial plugboard. Phase 2 does not
replace those assignments. It evolves only letters not present in the locked
mapping.

The search parameters are fixed in the source:

  Population size : 200
  Generations     : 300
  Mutation rate   : 0.25

### 7.2 Chromosome Representation

A chromosome is:

  { plugboard map, fitness score }

Its fitness is obtained by decrypting the entire ciphertext with the candidate
plugboard and passing the result to the N-gram plus IoC scoring engine.

### 7.3 Initialization

The first chromosome is exactly the locked Bombe mapping.

Each remaining chromosome begins with the same locked mapping and receives
between one and four mutation operations. This creates a local cloud around the
constraint-derived solution rather than a population scattered across the full
Steckerbrett universe.

### 7.4 Mutation Semantics

A mutation selects two unlocked letters a and b. Existing pairings involving
those selected letters are removed, and a new symmetric pair is installed:

  S(a) = b
  S(b) = a

Former partners become implicitly unconnected unless they are paired again by a
later mutation.

The operator respects the locked mapping by excluding every locked letter from
the mutation pool.

### 7.5 Elitism and Parent Selection

At every generation, the population is sorted by descending fitness.

The next generation begins with:

* The best solution ever observed.
* Nineteen additional high-ranking chromosomes.

The remaining population is produced by cloning a randomly selected chromosome
from the best half of the current population. Each clone mutates with
probability 0.25 and is then rescored.

There is no crossover operator in v16.

This matters. The phase is best described as a mutation-based evolutionary
search with elitism, not as a classical crossover-heavy Genetic Algorithm.

### 7.6 Exploration Versus Exploitation

Elitism preserves discovered structure. Top-half parent selection exploits the
best known region. Random mutation injects controlled disorder.

The process is stochastic and has no proof of global optimality. Its advantage
comes from entering the search after electrical constraints and Ringstellung
ranking have already compressed the battlefield.

Darwin is no longer sent into the entire jungle. Bombe hands him a map.

===========================================================================
CHAPTER VIII : THE HUMAN-IN-THE-LOOP OVERRIDE
===========================================================================

After the evolutionary stage, the program displays:

* The current plugboard pairs.
* The current plaintext candidate in five-letter groups.

The operator may accept the result by pressing ENTER or typing PERFEKT. The
operator may also enter a two-letter pair such as HJ.

When a manual pair is entered, any existing connections involving those two
letters are removed and the new symmetric pair is installed. The plaintext is
then decrypted and displayed again.

This phase acknowledges an uncomfortable truth: a heuristic scorer can prefer a
linguistically dense wrong answer over a semantically correct answer. Human
pattern recognition remains a valid final instrument, especially when the
message is short, abbreviated, misspelled, or outside the embedded dictionary.

The automated system proposes. The analyst disposes.

===========================================================================
CHAPTER IX : VITERBI-STYLE DYNAMIC PROGRAMMING FOR WORD SEGMENTATION
===========================================================================

### 9.1 The Problem

Enigma traffic is processed as continuous letters. Even a correct decryption
may appear as:

  DASOBERKOMMANDODERWEHRMACHT

The segmentation stage attempts to reconstruct:

  DAS OBERKOMMANDO DER WEHRMACHT

### 9.2 The Trellis

Let D[i] be the best segmentation score for the prefix ending at character i.
The implementation considers candidate words ending at i with a maximum length
of twenty characters:

  k belongs to [max(0, i - 20), i - 1]

For candidate word w = text[k:i], the recurrence is:

  D[i] = MAX_k ( D[k] + Emission(w) + Transition(previous, w) )

Backpointers record the winning previous position. Traversing those
backpointers from the end of the text reconstructs the selected word sequence.

### 9.3 Emission Heuristic

The emission score is:

  Emission(w) = 12 x |w|^3,   if w is in the normalized dictionary

  Emission(w) = -(|w| + 3),   otherwise

Long recognized words therefore receive a strong reward. Unknown fragments are
allowed, but penalized.

### 9.4 Transition Heuristic

The transition term uses word lengths rather than a learned word-to-word
probability matrix:

* Two consecutive one-letter segments receive -15.
* Two consecutive segments longer than three letters receive +2.
* All other transitions receive 0.

This discourages fragmentation and gives a small preference to sequences of
substantial words.

### 9.5 Complexity and Terminology

Because the look-back window is capped at twenty and dictionary lookup uses an
unordered_set, the implemented runtime is approximately:

  O(20N) = O(N)

with O(N) memory for scores and backpointers.

The routine is Viterbi-style because it computes a maximum-scoring path through
a dynamic-programming trellis. It is not a fully trained Hidden Markov Model:
there are no learned emission probabilities, no learned transition matrix, and
no explicit hidden-state vocabulary graph.

Accuracy begins with accurate names.

