===========================================================================
CHAPTER XI : FAILURE MODES, LIMITATIONS, AND SCIENTIFIC HONESTY
===========================================================================

### 11.1 Crib Dependence

The Phase-1 search depends on a correct or sufficiently useful crib. A wrong
crib can produce zero hits, misleading hits, or an explosion of candidates.
The predefined Offset-0 list accelerates a favorable case but does not prove
that real traffic begins with any listed phrase.

### 11.2 Heuristic Stop Selection

The thresholds of twelve assigned letters and one-to-five valid seed guesses
are engineering filters. They are not universal theorems of Enigma
cryptanalysis. Different crib lengths and menu structures may require different
selectivity.

### 11.3 Partial Ring Search Architecture

Phase 1 varies the right ring, while Phase 1.5 varies the left and middle rings.
This decomposition is an implementation strategy, not a claim that all ring
coordinates are cryptanalytically independent.

### 11.4 Language Bias

The fitness engine rewards N-grams found in the embedded vocabulary. Messages
containing names, code words, transcription errors, abbreviations, dialect,
foreign words, or vocabulary absent from the dictionary may score poorly even
when correctly decrypted.

### 11.5 No Historical Plug Count Enforcement

The plugboard map is represented as a symmetric partial permutation. The code
does not enforce one fixed historical number of cable pairs during the
evolutionary stage.

### 11.6 Stochastic Refinement

The evolutionary phase uses a time-seeded Mersenne Twister. Repeated runs may
produce different plugboard candidates and different final scores.

### 11.7 Candidate Representation

When multiple seed guesses survive a Bombe-inspired core test, the function
returns the last valid partial mapping rather than preserving every surviving
mapping as a separate candidate. This keeps the implementation compact, but it
can discard alternative branches.

### 11.8 Output Is a Candidate, Not a Proof

A high N-gram score, plausible IoC, readable segmentation, and attractive PDF do
not mathematically certify a decryption. Confidence should come from agreement
between the crib, recovered settings, plugboard consistency, full-message
semantics, and independent reproduction.

A green terminal is not peer review.

===========================================================================
CHAPTER XII : THE HYBRID PRINCIPLE
===========================================================================

The central scientific idea of v16 is algorithmic specialization.

The electrical stage answers:

  "Can this crib, core state, and partial plugboard coexist without
   contradiction?"

The Ringstellung stage answers:

  "Which electrically surviving key produces the strongest global language
   structure?"

The evolutionary stage answers:

  "Which additional unlocked plugboard mutations improve that structure?"

The human stage answers:

  "Does the result make operational and semantic sense?"

The segmentation stage answers:

  "Where are the most plausible word boundaries?"

No single component carries the full attack. Each component removes a different
kind of uncertainty.

Constraint propagation removes impossibility.
N-gram scoring removes linguistic noise.
Evolution removes local plugboard uncertainty.
Human review removes model blindness.
Dynamic programming removes spacing ambiguity.

That is the v16 architecture.

===========================================================================
CONCLUSION : FROM RANDOM SEARCH TO CONSTRAINED INFERENCE
===========================================================================

The previous design placed evolutionary search near the entrance of the maze.
Version 16 moves it closer to the exit.

First, the program enumerates every supported rotor order. Then it scans
27,418,560 core states with native CPU workers. At each state, it converts the
crib into involutive plugboard equations and propagates them until contradiction
or stabilization. Surviving configurations enter a 676-way left-and-middle
Ringstellung search, ranked by an IoC-gated dictionary-derived N-gram score.
Only then does a 200-member, 300-generation mutation engine refine the unlocked
plugboard letters.

The final output passes through a human override loop, a bounded Viterbi-style
segmenter, and a simulated historical report generator.

This is not pure brute force.
This is not pure Bombe logic.
This is not a classical Genetic Algorithm.
This is not a trained Markov model.
This is not a full Hidden Markov Model.

It is a hybrid constraint engine built from deterministic simulation,
combinatorial pruning, heuristic language features, stochastic refinement,
dynamic programming, and analyst judgment.

The cipher is not defeated by one giant hammer.
It is dismantled by a sequence of smaller tools, each shaped for one bolt.

